  "codebook": {
    "clarification": "Include as credit-claiming: (a) announcements that federal money, grants, projects, designations, disaster declarations, military missions, or agency services are coming to the senator's state or constituents, even without an explicit 'I secured it' statement, because a senator's own office announcing the benefit claims association with it; (b) explicit claims of responsibility, such as 'I helped secure,' 'my amendment,' 'legislation I authored,' 'funding I requested,' or 'at my request'; (c) claims that the senator's action (a letter, appeal, request, or vote) prompted or influenced a favorable agency decision, when the release links the two; (d) touting passage/adoption of the senator's own bill, amendment, or provision by a committee or chamber as an accomplishment; (e) reminders of past delivered benefits (e.g., prior funding wins, enacted provisions the senator authored). The credited benefit may be particularized (a grant to one town) or broader (a formula change benefiting the state), and may include claimed results of the senator's oversight or investigations.",
    "definition": "A press release is credit-claiming when the senator claims — explicitly or implicitly — personal or shared responsibility for a government action, outcome, or benefit that is delivered to (or protected for) their state, district, constituents, or a favored group. This includes announcing the senator secured or delivered federal funds, grants, projects, designations, disaster relief, base/mission decisions, or agency actions benefiting constituents, as well as claiming credit for enacted or chamber-passed legislation, adopted amendments, or provisions the senator authored, sponsored, or championed.",
    "negative_clarification": "Do not code as credit-claiming: (a) pure position-taking — statements of support or opposition, vote explanations, criticism of the administration or other parties, op-eds, and floor statements on issues; (b) introductions, sponsorships, or cosponsorships of bills that have not advanced, and advocacy urging future action (letters, requests, hearings, meetings with officials) where no favorable outcome is claimed; (c) ceremonial and commemorative content — condolences, tributes, remembrances, awards to others, essay contests, town-meeting announcements; (d) praise of collective or party accomplishments (e.g., 'Republican policies') without a personal claim; (e) oversight criticism or calls for reform where the senator claims no delivered result. Background mentions of committee seats, past participation, or priorities ('a priority of mine,' 'I will continue to fight') without asserting responsibility for a concrete delivered outcome are not sufficient.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "656",
        "explanation": "Kerry attacks the administration's port-security record after the Dubai ports deal collapsed; he takes a position and assigns blame but claims no responsibility for any benefit or outcome."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "57",
        "explanation": "Kennedy introduces and argues for stem-cell legislation. Introducing a bill that has not passed is advocacy/position-taking; no benefit has been delivered for which he can claim credit."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "918",
        "explanation": "Allard merely announces a town meeting; this is advertising/constituency outreach with no claim of any government benefit."
      }
    ],
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "2318",
        "explanation": "Durbin explicitly claims personal responsibility for federal actions benefiting an Illinois project: 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review' and 'facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding.'"
      },
